### RUN ##
To run them in parallel:
  bzt test-suite.yml

To run them sequentially (e.g., run Pytest to ensure functionality works,
then run JMeter for load testing):
  bzt test-suite.yml -sequential

### RESULTS ##
Viewing ResultsOnce you execute the command, Taurus launches a live, real-time ASCII
dashboard in your terminal. It provides metrics like active users, response times, 
error rates, and throughput.   When the tests finish, Taurus generates an artifacts/
folder containing detailed logs for both tools, including Pytest's standard output (pytest.out),
JMeter's raw data (kpi.jtl), and a consolidated execution summary (bzt.log).

---
execution:
  # 1. JMeter Execution Block (Performance Testing)
  - executor: jmeter
    concurrency: 50         # Number of concurrent virtual users
    ramp-up: 30s            # Time to reach target concurrency
    hold-for: 2m            # How long to sustain the load
    scenario: jmeter-test

  # 2. Pytest Execution Block (Functional Testing)
  - executor: pytest
    iterations: 1           # Perform 1 iteration over the test suite
    scenario: pytest-test

scenarios:
  jmeter-test:
    # You can point to an existing JMeter .jmx file here...
    script: path/to/your/load_test.jmx
    # ...OR write HTTP requests directly in YAML without opening JMeter
    # requests:
    #  - url: http://example.com/api

  pytest-test:
    # Point to a single Python file, or a folder containing your tests
    script: path/to/your/tests/test_api.py


### Parameters ######
In an enterprise or production environment, the standard practice is to use a combination of
JMeter Properties (with defaults) for configuration routing, and OS Environment Variables for sensitive data.

You should completely avoid hardcoding environment URLs, thread counts,
or passwords inside either the .jmx file or the test-suite.yml file.



### Production-Ready Architecture Example
Here is how a standard production workflow looks when executed
 through a CI/CD pipeline (e.g., GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or GitLab CI).

Step 1: The CI/CD Pipeline executes the test
The pipeline injects secrets and sets the load parameters dynamically
based on the environment (e.g., staging vs. production).


# Executed by your CI/CD runner
export API_TOKEN="<injected_from_secret_manager>"
export TARGET_ENV="api.production.com"
export VUSERS="1000"
export DURATION="3600"

bzt taurus_test_suite.yml
bzt taurus_test_suite.yml --debug


How to set it up:

Right-click your Test Plan > Add > Config Element > User Defined Variables.

Add your variables and set their values using the __P function, placing your desired manual 
GUI default on the right side:

Name: TARGET_HOST | Value: ${__P(host, manual-test-env.example.com)}
Name: TARGET_PORT | Value: ${__P(port, 443)}

Throughout the rest of your script (like in your HTTP Request), stop writing the __P function. Instead, use the clean variables you just created:

Server Name or IP: ${TARGET_HOST}
Port Number: ${TARGET_PORT}

Why this works perfectly for your workflow:

When testing manually in the GUI: JMeter evaluates ${TARGET_HOST}, sees no external property was passed,
 and uses manual-test-env.example.com. If you want to manually test a different host, you just change that
  one cell in the User Defined Variables table instead of hunting through every HTTP request.

When running via Taurus: 
Taurus passes the host property from your YAML file. The User Defined Variables component catches it, 
ignores the manual GUI default, and updates ${TARGET_HOST} globally for the entire test run.